The Caprini-Lellouch-Neubert (CLN) and the Boyd-Goornstein-Lebed (BGL) frameworks both aim to relate $D_s^\pm 	o \gamma l^\pm  ar{
u}$ and $D^\pm 	o \gamma l^\pm  ar{
u}$ decay rates to form factors at low energies, but they diverge significantly in their extrapolation to high energies and in the constraints they place on the shape parameters of the $D_s^\pm$ and $D^\pm$ mesons' photon wavefunctions. A theoretical analysis of these decays using the BGL and CLN models reveals that while both are consistent with existing experimental data, the BGL model aligns better with QCD sum function predictions for the decay constants of $D_s^\pm$ and $D^\pm$ mesons. A novel, model-independent dispersion relation is proposed to link $D_s^\pm 	o \gamma l^\pm  ar{
u}$ form factors to the structure function of the $D_s^\pm$ meson, and using both the BGL and CLN models, the research estimates the decay rate due to $D_s^\pm \gamma$ and $D_s^\pm l  ar{
u}$ decays.
